INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-01', 'Why usage statistics lie', 'assessment', $$REF-01 — Why usage statistics lie: usage extracts are necessary evidence and insufficient proof. A view with zero interactive opens in 180 days is a candidate for retirement, never a confirmed one, because every BI platform has consumption paths that never register as a view. Subscriptions and email delivery are the largest gap: Tableau subscriptions, Power BI subscriptions and Paginated Report delivery, and BusinessObjects scheduled instances send a PDF or image to an inbox, and the recipient never touches the server. Embedded views inside an EHR portal, a SharePoint page, or an intranet tile are often recorded under a service account rather than the human reader, so a view with one "user" may have two hundred. API and extract consumers (a Python job hitting a Tableau data source, a Power BI dataset queried through XMLA, a Webi document refreshed by a downstream ETL step) show up as refreshes, not reads. Cached PDFs live on a shared drive and are reopened for months. Shared logins collapse a whole unit into one user id. Seasonal and annual reports (the cost report workpapers, the annual EEO-1 submission, the year-end bad debt review) legitimately show nothing for eleven months. The workbench handles each gap explicitly. Subscription and schedule tables are loaded alongside view events and a subscribed asset cannot receive RETIRE from a usage rule alone. Service-account readers are flagged in RAW_USAGE and their assets are routed to INVESTIGATE. Extract and refresh events are counted as a separate consumer class. The lookback for usage rules is at least 13 months so annual cycles land inside it. And most importantly, a low-usage disposition is a recommendation that starts an owner notification and a 30-day silence window; it never deletes anything. The honest answer to "is anybody using this" is "nobody opened it interactively in the window we could observe, and here is what we could not observe."$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-02', 'Disposition vocabulary', 'rationalization', $$REF-02 — Disposition vocabulary: the rule engine assigns exactly one of six dispositions to every asset in the catalog, and each has a precise operational meaning. RETIRE means the asset will not exist in the future state: no readers in the lookback, no subscription, no regulatory obligation, no downstream dependency, and the owner has been notified. Operationally it means the asset is moved to an archive project, exported once to a static file, and deleted at platform decommission. CONSOLIDATE means the asset is a member of a duplicate cluster and is not the canonical member; its distinctive requirements (an extra filter, a different grain, a regional breakdown) are captured as requirements against the canonical, and the asset itself is retired when the canonical is live in Sigma. MIGRATE means the asset survives as-is in function and its logic is converted: expressions run through the rule catalog and the residual pass, then reconcile against the certified mart. REBUILD means the asset survives in function but not in implementation: the logic is too tangled, or the source is being replaced, so it is rebuilt directly on a certified dataset and only its outputs are reconciled. SELF_SERVICE means the asset is a one-reader or one-author analysis that should not be an engineering deliverable; the author is enabled on the governed dataset and builds it themselves. INVESTIGATE means the evidence conflicts (a service-account reader, a title that matches a regulatory term with no register entry, a data source the catalog cannot resolve) and a named person must look. A recommendation is not a decision. Every disposition sits in REVIEW_QUEUE with a rule id, an evidence JSON, an owner notification timestamp, and a reviewer field. RETIRE and CONSOLIDATE additionally require the 30-day silence window to elapse without an owner objection. Nothing reaches APPROVED without a named reviewer, and the reviewer is a client employee, not a consultant and not the copilot.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-03', 'Duplicate detection and canonical selection', 'rationalization', $$REF-03 — Duplicate detection and canonical selection: title similarity finds the easy duplicates and misses the expensive ones. "Daily Census" and "Daily Census v2 FINAL" are obviously related, but "ED Throughput" and "Emergency Department Operations Scorecard" can be the same report built twice in different departments, and "Denials Dashboard" and "Denials Dashboard" in two Tableau projects can be entirely different metrics. The workbench matches on signature, not name. A signature is the set of source entities an asset reads (from lineage), the set of metric expressions it computes (normalized: whitespace stripped, aliases resolved, field names mapped to catalog columns), and its grain (the dimensions at the lowest level of any visual). Two assets with Jaccard overlap above threshold on entities and metrics, and the same grain, are placed in one cluster; title similarity is only a tiebreaker. Within a cluster a canonical must be chosen, and the order is fixed: a certified or governed asset beats anything, then the most-read asset by distinct human readers in the lookback, then the most recently maintained by a non-service account. The order is deliberate: readership is a better signal of trust than recency, and certification beats both because someone already took responsibility for it. The members that are not canonical are not simply thrown away. The diff between a member and the canonical (an additional filter on payer class, a facility breakdown, a trailing twelve-month window instead of fiscal year to date) is written to CLUSTER_REQUIREMENTS, and those requirements are attached to the canonical's conversion or rebuild ticket. A consolidation that loses a requirement is a failed consolidation; the orphaned reader rebuilds the old report in a spreadsheet and the estate has not shrunk.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-04', 'The regulatory and obligation register', 'rationalization', $$REF-04 — The regulatory and obligation register: a US health system produces a set of reports whose usage pattern looks exactly like a dead report and whose absence produces a fine, a failed audit, or a withheld payment. The Medicare cost report workpapers are assembled once a year. The EEO-1 submission is annual. NHSN surveillance submissions are monthly but read by two people. Core measure and eCQM abstractions are quarterly. Medical loss ratio filings for a provider-sponsored plan are annual. ACO quality and financial submissions are annual with a narrow window. Add the ones that are contractual rather than statutory: a payer's value-based contract reconciliation, a bond covenant reporting package, the 340B audit trail, a state all-payer claims database submission. Every one of these can show zero interactive views for most of the year and every one is mandatory. The register is a table, REGULATORY_REGISTER, maintained by the client's compliance and finance leads, that maps each obligation to the assets that produce it, the cadence, the owner, and the authority (CMS, EEOC, CDC, state, contract). The rule engine evaluates the register before any usage rule, and an asset that matches the register is pinned to MIGRATE or REBUILD regardless of usage; the usage rules never see it. The register also catches near-misses through INVESTIGATE: an asset whose title or columns match a regulatory vocabulary (cost report, worksheet, HCRIS, CMS-2552, NHSN, HEDIS, MLR) but that has no register entry is routed to a human rather than to a disposition. The register is the first thing the engagement asks the client to complete and the thing most likely to be incomplete on the first pass, which is why the vocabulary match exists. A rationalization that retires a cost report input is not a cost saving.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-05', 'Complexity scoring and effort estimation', 'assessment', $$REF-05 — Complexity scoring and effort estimation: the cost of converting a report is driven by its logic, not its visual count. The scoring policy assigns points for constructs that historically consume engineering time. In Tableau: level-of-detail expressions (FIXED is moderate, INCLUDE and EXCLUDE are expensive because they depend on visual context), table calculations with addressing and partitioning, custom SQL data sources, data blending across published sources, and parameters that drive calculation logic. In Power BI: DAX time intelligence, CALCULATE with multiple filter modifiers, iterator measures over large tables, bidirectional relationships, calculated tables, and row-level security roles. In BusinessObjects: calculation contexts (In, ForEach, ForAll), merged dimensions across data providers, universe-level derived tables, and Where() operators nested in aggregates. Across all platforms: the number of distinct source systems, any spreadsheet or flat-file source, and the sheer count of distinct calculated fields. Points sum into bands: S (under 8 points, a day or less), M (8 to 20, two to four days), L (20 to 45, one to two weeks), XL (above 45, a rebuild candidate by default). The hours per band are a starting policy, not a measurement. They are marked TO CONFIRM until the first wave ships, at which point actual hours per converted asset are written back to EFFORT_ACTUALS and the band hours are recalibrated. Do not quote total estate effort to an executive before the first wave has been measured; quote the band distribution and the calibration plan instead. The policy thresholds live in SCORING_POLICY and are client-editable; the scoring function is not.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-06', 'Why rebuild beats converting complex logic as-is', 'rationalization', $$REF-06 — Why rebuild beats converting complex logic as-is: a workbook with forty calculated fields, three LOD expressions, two blended data sources, and a custom SQL query is usually not forty requirements. It is three or four business questions that an author answered by working around the limits of the data they had. The custom SQL exists because the published source was missing a column. The LOD exists because the grain was wrong. The blend exists because two sources were never joined upstream. Converting that logic faithfully into Sigma reproduces every workaround, at full conversion cost, on a platform that no longer has the constraint that caused it. The REBUILD disposition says: identify the business questions, confirm the certified dataset in the target model answers them at the right grain, and build the asset fresh on that dataset. Most of the forty fields become columns that already exist, and the handful that remain are simple formulas on a grouped table. Reconciliation still applies, but it reconciles outputs (the numbers the readers see) rather than intermediate expressions. The scoring policy routes XL assets to REBUILD by default and L assets to REVIEW for a human choice. The exception is an asset with a documented, audited calculation (a regulatory measure with a published specification) where fidelity to the exact formula matters more than simplicity; those stay MIGRATE and the conversion is traced line by line. The rebuild decision is where the target model earns its keep: if the certified dataset cannot answer the questions, that is a model gap to fix, not a reason to convert the workaround.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-07', 'Wave planning by subject area', 'planning', $$REF-07 — Wave planning by subject area: migrations fail when they move reports in the order someone asked for them. Waves are cut by subject area because subject areas share dimensions, and a wave that ships a subject area ships a coherent certified dataset and every surviving asset that reads it. Wave 0 is the foundation: the shared conformed dimensions (patient, encounter, provider, facility, department, payer, date, cost center) and the security model, built and certified before any fact table. No report ships in wave 0 and that is correct; everything after depends on it. Subsequent waves are ordered by a weighted combination of distinct human readers, executive consumers (a report read by the CFO or COO is ordered ahead of one read by twenty analysts), and obligation deadlines from the register (a wave must land before the next submission window it supports). Within a subject area the wave is then cut by effort: the sum of band hours for the surviving assets must fit the wave's capacity, and if it does not, the lowest-readership MIGRATE assets are deferred, never the obligations. A wave's exit criteria are specific. The subject area's certified dataset is published in Sigma with a named data owner. Every MIGRATE asset in the wave has a reconciliation status of PASS or an accepted DEFINITION_VARIANCE with business sign-off. Every REBUILD asset has output reconciliation signed. Every CONSOLIDATE member's requirements are either satisfied by the canonical or explicitly declined by the requester. Readers of the source assets have been notified of the Sigma location and the read-only freeze date for the legacy copies. If any criterion is open, the wave is not closed, and the next wave does not start on the same dataset.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-08', 'Deriving the target model from lineage', 'target-model', $$REF-08 — Deriving the target model from lineage: the future-state model is not designed in a workshop and then mapped; it is derived from what the surviving assets actually read and then refined. The inputs are lineage from the source platforms: the Tableau Metadata API gives workbook to data source to database table and column; Power BI's scanner API gives dataset tables, columns, measures, and their upstream sources; the BusinessObjects CMS and universe designer exports give objects to universe tables to database columns. The derivation is a set of joins. Entities come from the source catalog (SOURCE_CATALOG), which resolves every referenced physical table to a business entity and a system (Epic Clarity, the ERP, the payer claims feed, a departmental spreadsheet). Attributes come from referenced columns: a column appears in the target model only if at least one surviving asset (disposition MIGRATE, REBUILD, or CONSOLIDATE canonical) references it, directly or through a calculated field. Retired and self-service assets do not contribute; this is the single largest reason the target model is smaller than the source estate. Shared dimensions are identified by the same entity appearing in more than one subject area's lineage, and they are promoted to conformed dimensions in the foundation wave. Grain is inferred from the lowest-level dimension combination any surviving visual uses against a fact, then confirmed by a human, because a visual can hide a grain behind an aggregation. The output is TARGET_MODEL (entities, attributes, grain, source mapping status) and it is reviewed by the client's data architects, who add what lineage cannot see: slowly changing dimension policy, surrogate keys, and the attributes that the new system of record will carry that the old one never did. The model is derived so that the review starts from evidence rather than from a blank page.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-09', 'Metric reconciliation: one term, many formulas', 'metrics', $$REF-09 — Metric reconciliation: one term, many formulas. The most expensive discovery in a BI migration is that the estate agrees on names and disagrees on arithmetic. Net patient revenue is computed in some workbooks as gross charges less contractual allowances, in others additionally less bad debt and charity, and the two are tens of millions apart at a mid-size system. Length of stay is days between admit and discharge dates in one report, hours divided by 24 in another, and patient days divided by discharges in a third; the third is an average with a different denominator and is not comparable to the first two at all. Thirty-day readmission rate uses index admissions as the denominator in one place (CMS style, excluding planned readmissions and deaths) and all discharges in another. Denial rate is counted by claim count in revenue cycle reports and by dollars in finance reports, and both are called "denial rate." PMPM divides by member months in the actuarial reports and by distinct members in the operations dashboards. Overtime percentage divides by paid hours in HR and by productive hours in nursing. Budget variance is actual minus budget in one department and budget minus actual in another, so a favorable expense variance is positive in one and negative in the other. The workbench extracts every metric expression, normalizes it, and groups expressions by business term into METRIC_VARIANTS. Variants are marked EQUIVALENT when they differ only syntactically (the rule catalog proves this) and DIVERGENT when they differ semantically. A divergent group is not resolved by the engineer or by the copilot. It is resolved by certification: a named business owner (the CFO's delegate for revenue metrics, the CNO's for nursing, the CMO's for quality) selects or writes the certified definition, which is recorded in CERTIFIED_METRICS with their name and date. Every non-certified variant then reconciles against the certified one and the difference is reported as DEFINITION_VARIANCE, not as an error.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-10', 'Source-to-target mapping statuses', 'target-model', $$REF-10 — Source-to-target mapping statuses: every attribute in TARGET_MODEL carries a mapping status that tells the conversion and rebuild teams where the data will come from in the future state, and the statuses are deliberately few. MAPPED means the source column resolves to a physical column in the certified mart on Snowflake, with a documented transformation if any, and conversion can proceed. SOURCE_DECOMMISSIONED means the column comes from a system that is being replaced during or before the migration (a legacy practice management system being folded into the enterprise EHR, a homegrown cost accounting tool being replaced by a vendor product). For these, the attribute is mapped to the replacement system's equivalent, and the mapping carries the cutover date; assets that read decommissioned sources are routed to REBUILD rather than MIGRATE because the expression is written against a schema that will not exist. SPREADSHEET_SOURCE means lineage traces to an Excel or CSV file, which is common for budgets, targets, staffing plans, payer contract terms, and manual adjustments. A spreadsheet source is resolved one of two ways and the mapping records which. Either a system of record is identified that the spreadsheet was shadowing (the budget lives in the ERP; the spreadsheet was a copy), and the attribute maps there; or the spreadsheet is genuinely the only home for the data, and it becomes a governed upload: a Snowflake table loaded through a controlled process (a Sigma input table, a validated file load to a stage, or a simple form) with an owner, a schema, and a load history. An ungoverned spreadsheet on a shared drive is never a valid future-state source; a report that depends on one is BLOCKED until the mapping is resolved. The statuses are visible in the copilot so an engineer asking "why is this report blocked" gets the attribute and the spreadsheet path, not a guess.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-11', 'Sigma concepts for Tableau, Power BI and BO people', 'sigma', $$REF-11 — Sigma concepts for Tableau, Power BI and BO people: Sigma is organized as workbooks containing pages containing elements. An element is a table, a pivot, a visualization, a control, or a text block. The closest analogies are a Tableau workbook with dashboards and sheets, or a Power BI report with pages and visuals, but the table element is the primary object in Sigma in a way it is not elsewhere: charts are built from a table's columns, and the table is where formulas live. Data elements connect to a data source. A data source can be a Snowflake table or view directly, or a Sigma dataset, which is a saved, reusable, optionally certified query layer with its own columns and formulas. Certified datasets are the Sigma equivalent of a Tableau published and certified data source, a Power BI certified dataset, or a BusinessObjects universe, and the target model's certified marts are exposed as exactly this. Grouping levels replace most of what Tableau does with LOD expressions and Power BI does with filter context. A table can have multiple grouping levels (for example Facility, then Department, then row detail), and a formula at a grouping level aggregates the rows beneath it. A formula can reference a column at another grouping level with the [Grouping/Column] syntax, which is how a row-level formula reads its group's total. Controls are Sigma's parameters and filters: list, date range, number range, text, and switch controls, which can be targeted at elements and referenced in formulas by name. Formulas are written in Sigma's function language, and every formula is compiled to SQL and executed in Snowflake at query time. There are no extracts, no imports, no scheduled refreshes of a cached model; the data is as current as the Snowflake table. This is the single biggest mental shift for Tableau extract users and Power BI import-mode users, and it removes an entire class of staleness defects.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-12', 'Tableau to Sigma conversion guide', 'conversion', $$REF-12 — Tableau to Sigma conversion guide: most Tableau calculated fields convert mechanically. Function names map nearly one to one for math, string, and date functions: SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, COUNT, COUNTD (to CountDistinct), DATEDIFF, DATEADD, DATETRUNC, LEFT, RIGHT, MID (to Substring), CONTAINS, UPPER, LOWER, TRIM, ROUND, ABS. Field references change quoting: Tableau's [Field Name] is also Sigma's [Field Name], but Sigma does not use the [Parameters].[Name] form; a parameter becomes a control referenced as [Control Name]. IF/ELSEIF/ELSE/END converts to If(cond, then, cond, then, else), and CASE converts to Switch(expr, val, result, val, result, default). IIF converts to If. ATTR has no equivalent and usually means the author wanted a dimension at a grouping level; move the column to the grouping. ZN(x) converts to Coalesce(x, 0). ISNULL converts to IsNull. Level-of-detail expressions are the main judgment call. {FIXED [Dim] : SUM([Measure])} converts to a grouped child table or a grouping level keyed on Dim, with the SUM as a formula at that level, then referenced from row detail as [Dim Group/Measure Sum]. When the FIXED dimension matches a filter or condition, SumIf([Measure], [Dim] = value) is often simpler. INCLUDE and EXCLUDE depend on the visual's dimensions and have no direct form; they are rewritten per visual as grouping-level formulas, and each is flagged for REVIEW. Table calculations convert to Sigma's window functions on a sorted table: RUNNING_SUM to CumulativeSum, LOOKUP(x, -1) to Lag(x, 1), RANK to Rank, WINDOW_SUM with a moving range to MovingSum, and percent-of-total to x / [Grouping/Total]. Addressing and partitioning become the table's sort and grouping, which must be set explicitly. Custom SQL data sources convert to Snowflake views in the certified schema, not to Sigma SQL elements, so they are governed. Data blends convert to dataset joins or, more often, to a fact table that already has the join.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-13', 'Power BI and DAX to Sigma conversion guide', 'conversion', $$REF-13 — Power BI and DAX to Sigma conversion guide: DAX measures are formulas that evaluate inside a filter context supplied by the visual, and Sigma's equivalent is a formula on a grouped table where the grouping supplies the context. A simple measure such as Total Revenue = SUM(Charges[Amount]) becomes Sum([Amount]) at whatever grouping level the visual needs. CALCULATE(SUM(x), Table[Col] = "value") becomes SumIf([x], [Col] = "value"); CALCULATE(COUNTROWS(t), filter) becomes CountIf(filter) or CountDistinctIf for distinct keys. The filter-context modifiers are the ones that need thought. ALL(Table[Col]) inside CALCULATE removes the filter on that column and is used for share-of-total math; in Sigma that is a reference to a higher grouping level, [Total/Amount Sum], or to a column at the table's top level. ALLSELECTED is the same pattern but respects controls, which is the default behavior of a grouping-level total in Sigma when controls are applied to the element. REMOVEFILTERS follows ALL. Time intelligence converts to explicit date-dimension arithmetic rather than to magic functions: TOTALYTD(measure, Date[Date]) becomes SumIf([Amount], [Fiscal Year] = [Selected Fiscal Year] and [Date] <= [Selected Date]); SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR becomes the same SumIf shifted with DateAdd("year", -1, ...) on the date dimension; DATESINPERIOD for trailing windows becomes a date-range condition or MovingSum over a date-sorted table. This is more verbose and much easier to audit. Iterators convert by materializing the row-level expression: SUMX(Table, expr) is a row-level column for expr and a Sum at the grouping level; AVERAGEX likewise with Avg; RANKX becomes Rank on the grouped table with an explicit sort. VAR/RETURN blocks are inlined or split into named helper columns. Table[Column] references become [Column] when the table is the element's source, or a dataset join when it is not. Row-level security roles convert to Snowflake row access policies on the certified tables, or to Sigma user attributes when the filtering is presentation-level only; the policy is preferred because it holds for every consumer, not just Sigma. Import mode and incremental refresh have no equivalent and need none.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-14', 'SAP BusinessObjects and Webi to Sigma conversion guide', 'conversion', $$REF-14 — SAP BusinessObjects and Webi to Sigma conversion guide: a BusinessObjects universe is already a semantic layer, so the conversion target for a universe is a certified Sigma dataset (or several, one per fact context) on the Snowflake mart, and universe objects become dataset columns. Dimension objects map to columns directly; measure objects with a projection function map to formulas with the corresponding aggregate; detail objects map to columns on the parent dimension's table. Universe contexts and aggregate awareness disappear, because the mart has one grain per fact and Snowflake handles the aggregation. Webi calculation contexts are the main conversion work. A formula with no context operator aggregates at the block's current level, which is a Sigma formula at the table's grouping level. In Report is the top-level total: [Total/Measure]. In ([Dim]) is a formula at a grouping level keyed on Dim. ForEach adds a dimension to the context and ForAll removes one, and both become choices of grouping level, with the caveat that ForAll on a dimension the block displays needs a higher grouping level referenced from the lower one. NoFilter bypasses report filters and is a formula on an element with no controls targeted, or a dataset-level formula. Count in Webi defaults to distinct, so Count([Object]) converts to CountDistinct, not Count; this is the most common silent error in BO conversions. Where() operators convert to conditional aggregates: Sum([Amount]) Where ([Payer] = "Medicare") becomes SumIf([Amount], [Payer] = "Medicare"), and Count([Encounter]) Where (...) becomes CountDistinctIf. Prompts convert to controls, and optional prompts convert to controls with a null default that the formula or filter treats as no filter. Merged dimensions across data providers convert to dataset joins on the merged key, or better, to a single dataset on the mart where the join already exists. Scheduled instances with email or file destinations convert to Sigma scheduled exports, and the destinations are carried into the subscription inventory so nobody loses a delivery. Publications with bursting profiles convert to scheduled exports with a control per recipient set, and are flagged REVIEW because the recipient logic is usually undocumented.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-15', 'When to use Claude for the residual', 'conversion', $$REF-15 — When to use Claude for the residual: the conversion rule catalog runs first and handles the majority of expressions deterministically: function renames, quoting, IF to If, ZN to Coalesce, simple LOD and CALCULATE patterns, and the window function family. A rule conversion is reproducible, explainable (it cites its rule id), and free. What remains is the residual: nested LOD expressions, DAX with several modifiers, Webi contexts combined with Where(), expressions that reference fields the catalog cannot resolve, and anything with embedded business logic that a rule cannot see. For the residual the workbench calls Claude through Cortex (SNOWFLAKE.CORTEX.COMPLETE with a structured response format) with the source expression, the source platform, the resolved field mappings, the target dataset's column list, and the Sigma function list that the client has approved. The output is structured, never free text: the proposed Sigma formula, a confidence score, a list of assumptions, a requires_human flag, and an explanation. The rules for using it are strict. Every formula it produces lands in CONVERSION_RESULTS with SOURCE = "AI" and STATUS = REVIEW; an AI-produced formula is never written to Sigma without an engineer opening it, and requires_human = true routes it to a senior reviewer regardless of confidence. The function list is passed in explicitly so the model cannot invent a function Sigma does not have, and a post-check rejects any formula that references a function or column outside the allowed lists. Cost is controlled in three ways: batch limits per run (the residual is converted in batches of fifty expressions with a run id, so a bad prompt does not burn the whole estate), a smaller and cheaper model for pattern-shaped work where the rules almost matched, and a stronger model reserved for expressions where the rules gave up entirely and judgment is required. Token counts per run are written to FIELD_CONVERSION (MODEL_USED, AI_CONFIDENCE) so the spend is visible in the same database as the work.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-16', 'Reconciliation and acceptance', 'testing', $$REF-16 — Reconciliation and acceptance: a converted or rebuilt asset is accepted when its numbers match the certified mart within a materiality threshold over a fixed comparison period, and a named business owner signs. Thresholds differ by metric type because the arithmetic differs. Currency metrics (net revenue, charges, payments, expense) use a relative threshold, typically 0.1 percent, with an absolute floor so a tiny base does not produce a false pass. Rates and percentages (readmission rate, denial rate, overtime percentage, margin) use an absolute threshold in percentage points, typically 0.05, because a relative threshold on a 2 percent rate is meaningless. Counts (encounters, discharges, members, claims) must match exactly; there is no material count difference, only a definition difference. The comparison period is fixed in advance (usually the last closed fiscal month and the same month a year prior), the source platform's data is frozen for that period, and both sides are run against the same snapshot. Results are one of three statuses. PASS: within threshold. FAIL: outside threshold and the cause is a conversion defect (wrong join, wrong filter, wrong function), which the engineer fixes and reruns. DEFINITION_VARIANCE: outside threshold and the cause is that the source used a non-certified metric definition (REF-09); the converted asset is correct against the certified definition and the source was not. The rule an engineer must internalize is that a DEFINITION_VARIANCE is never fixed by changing the conversion to match the source. Matching a wrong number is the fastest way to migrate an error into a new platform with a certification stamp on it. Instead the variance is documented with both numbers, the certified definition, and the source definition, and the business owner either accepts the certified number (and the readers are told the number will change) or escalates to re-certify the metric. Sign-off is recorded in ACCEPTANCE_SIGNOFF with the owner's name, the date, the run id, and the status, and an asset without a signed acceptance is not in production regardless of what the Sigma workbook looks like.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-17', 'Conversion readiness states', 'conversion', $$REF-17 — Conversion readiness states: before an asset enters a conversion sprint it has a readiness state, computed from the state of its dependencies rather than from anyone's opinion. READY means every source column the asset references has mapping status MAPPED, every metric it computes is either certified or EQUIVALENT to a certified definition, the certified dataset for its subject area is published in Sigma, and the asset's disposition is APPROVED as MIGRATE or REBUILD. An engineer can pick up a READY asset and expect no external blockers. REVIEW means the asset can be converted but a human decision is pending on something inside it: an INCLUDE or EXCLUDE LOD, a DAX measure with several filter modifiers, a Webi publication with bursting, an AI-produced formula with requires_human set, or a duplicate-cluster requirement that has not been confirmed with the requester. REVIEW assets are pulled into sprints with the reviewer named and the review scheduled, and the conversion proceeds in parallel with the review for everything not affected by it. BLOCKED means the asset cannot be correctly converted yet, for a reason outside the conversion team's control: a source column with status SPREADSHEET_SOURCE that has no governed replacement, a SOURCE_DECOMMISSIONED column whose replacement system has not gone live, a DIVERGENT metric with no certified definition, a disposition still in the 30-day silence window, or a register obligation whose owner has not confirmed the specification. The state records the specific blocker and its owner. Unblocking is always a named action by a named person: the data owner resolves the spreadsheet, the metric owner certifies, the reviewer approves the disposition. The copilot can tell an engineer exactly why an asset is BLOCKED and who owns the unblock, and it will not change the state itself.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-18', 'Change management and author enablement', 'adoption', $$REF-18 — Change management and author enablement: a large fraction of any BI estate is analyses with one author and one reader, often the same person, and the cheapest correct thing to do with them is not to migrate them. The SELF_SERVICE disposition hands the author a certified dataset in Sigma, a short enablement session, and a deadline, and the author rebuilds what they still need. Most do not rebuild everything, which is the point; the analysis was a moment in time. Enablement is organized by subject area in step with the waves: when the revenue cycle dataset certifies, the revenue cycle analysts are trained on it that same week, using their own retired workbooks as the exercises. Training covers the Sigma concepts that differ (REF-11), the certified metric definitions and why they may differ from what the analyst computed before, and how to request a dataset change rather than working around it with a spreadsheet. Retirement runs in waves too. When a wave closes, the legacy copies of its assets go read-only on a published date, stay visible for a defined period with a banner pointing to the Sigma location, and are then archived. Archive means a static export (PDF or CSV of the last run, plus the workbook file) stored in a Snowflake stage or a governed document store with the asset id, so a future auditor can see what a report looked like, without the platform. Decommissioning the old platform is planned backward from the license renewal date: the final wave must close, the final read-only period must elapse, and the archive must be verified before the renewal decision, or the client pays another year for a platform that serves three reports. Subscriptions are the last thing to move and the first thing people notice if they break; the subscription inventory from the assessment is reconciled against Sigma scheduled exports before any read-only freeze.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-19', 'What the engagement changes at a client and what it never changes', 'method', $$REF-19 — What the engagement changes at a client and what it never changes: the workbench separates configuration, which is the client's and changes on every engagement, from the engine, which is the accelerator's and does not. Configuration the client owns and the engagement populates: the source catalog (SOURCE_CATALOG), which maps every physical table in lineage to a business entity and system and is the single most important input to the target model; the regulatory and obligation register (REGULATORY_REGISTER), which only the client's compliance and finance people can complete; the scoring policy thresholds (SCORING_POLICY), including band boundaries and hours per band, which are recalibrated after the first wave; the rule order and rule parameters (RULE_CONFIG), such as the usage lookback in months, the minimum distinct readers, the duplicate similarity threshold, and the length of the silence window; the conversion rule catalog (CONVERSION_RULE), which grows as the engagement meets new patterns and is the main reusable asset across clients; and the approved Sigma function list (SIGMA_FUNCTION), which bounds both the rule catalog and the residual pass. What the engagement never changes: the rule engine itself, which evaluates rules in configured order and writes a disposition with a rule id and evidence JSON; the duplicate signature algorithm; the lineage derivation joins that produce the target model; the reconciliation comparison logic and status assignment; and the review gate, which requires a named reviewer and cannot be bypassed by configuration. The distinction matters for trust. A client can read the rule order and the thresholds and understand every disposition from configuration alone, and a second engagement at a second client starts from the same engine with different configuration, so a defect fixed in the engine is fixed everywhere.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-20', 'Evidence, provenance and what the copilot will not do', 'governance', $$REF-20 — Evidence, provenance and what the copilot will not do: every row the workbench produces carries enough provenance to be challenged. A disposition in RATIONALIZATION_RESULTS has the rule id that fired, the rule's version, the run id, and an evidence JSON that contains the specific facts the rule used: distinct readers in the lookback and the lookback window, the subscription count, the register match or its absence, the cluster id and canonical, the complexity score and its component points. A reviewer or an auditor can recompute the disposition from the evidence without rerunning anything. Every conversion in CONVERSION_RESULTS has a SOURCE column that is RULE (with the rule id) or AI (with the model, the run id, the confidence, and the assumptions), and AI-sourced rows are labeled as such in every downstream view and in every copilot answer. Every reconciliation has the run id, the snapshot identifiers on both sides, the threshold applied, and the status. Every acceptance and every certification has a human name and a timestamp. The copilot reads all of this through Cortex Search over the reference corpus and Cortex Analyst over the result tables, and it answers "why" questions by citing the rule, the evidence, and the relevant reference document. The copilot is deliberately limited in what it will do. It will not retire an asset, change a disposition, or approve a review item; those are writes that require a named reviewer. It will not certify a metric definition; that is a business owner's act. It will not sign off a reconciliation or mark an asset accepted. It will not produce a Sigma formula as final; its formulas land in REVIEW like any other AI output. It will draft the owner notification for a candidate retirement, explain a variance, summarize a cluster's requirements, and point an engineer to the blocker's owner. When asked to do something it will not do, it says who can and where the action lives. An answer that cannot cite evidence says so rather than filling the gap.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-21', 'Snowflake as the data plane for the whole migration', 'architecture', $$REF-21 — Snowflake as the data plane for the whole migration: the assessment, rationalization, target model, conversion, and testing all live in one Snowflake database (BI_MODERNIZATION, with schemas RAW, ASSESS, RATIONALIZE, MODEL, CONVERT, TEST, and AI) and that is a deliberate design rather than a convenience. One security model: usage extracts, lineage, report titles, filter values, and reconciliation results are governed by the same roles, masking, and row access policies as the client's other data, and access is logged in ACCOUNT_USAGE rather than in a consultant's laptop. Cortex is available in place: Cortex Search indexes the reference corpus and the asset catalog, Cortex Analyst answers structured questions over the result tables through a semantic model, and COMPLETE runs the residual conversion, with no data leaving the account. Reproducibility: every stage is a SQL procedure with a run id, so a disposition or a reconciliation can be rerun against the same inputs and produce the same outputs, and a changed threshold produces a visible diff between runs. No exported spreadsheets: the traditional BI assessment is a consultant's workbook of report inventories passed around by email, and it is wrong within a week; here the inventory is a table, the review queue is a table, and the Sigma workbench and the copilot both read it live. A note on sensitivity: usage extracts and lineage are metadata, not PHI, and the workbench does not load report data. But report titles, filter values, and parameter defaults can carry patient names, MRNs, or diagnosis terms that an author typed into a filter, and reader identities are workforce data. Treat the whole database as internal, apply masking to reader identifiers for any role outside the migration team, and do not export it. The reconciliation stage does read the certified mart, and inherits its policies. The same Snowflake account that will serve Sigma in production serves the migration, so there is no second environment to secure or to decommission when the work is done.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-22', 'Usage extract mechanics per platform', 'assessment', $$REF-22 — Usage extract mechanics per platform: what the platforms actually provide determines what the rules can see. Tableau Server and Cloud expose the workgroup repository (or Admin Insights on Cloud) with views_stats, which is an aggregate of view counts per user per view, and historical_events, which is the event-level log of views, downloads, subscriptions, and extract refreshes with timestamps; historical_events is the one to load because it has time, user, and event type, while views_stats loses the time dimension. Subscriptions and schedules come from the subscriptions and tasks tables. Lineage comes from the Metadata API (GraphQL), which returns workbook, sheet, dashboard, published data source, embedded data source, database table, column, and the calculated field formulas, and is the single richest lineage source of the three platforms. Power BI provides activity events through the admin REST API (Get Activity Events) with a default retention of 30 days, which is the trap: if nobody has been exporting, the history does not exist, and a 13-month lookback cannot be reconstructed. Start a daily export into RAW on day one of the assessment and accept that the first lookback is short; Microsoft 365 audit logs sometimes extend it if the tenant has longer retention. The scanner API (WorkspaceInfo with lineage and datasource details) provides datasets, tables, columns, measures with DAX, and upstream data sources, and is lineage of good quality; report-to-visual-to-field lineage requires parsing PBIX or PBIP files. BusinessObjects provides the Auditing Data Store (ADS), a relational database of events with document, user, and action, whose retention depends on how it was configured and is frequently either off or purged; and the CMS repository, queryable through the CMS query language or the REST SDK, for document inventory, schedules, publications, and universe assignments. Webi formulas require opening each document through the SDK or parsing exported XML. Common gaps across all three: service-account readers, embedded views attributed to one identity, extract and API consumption recorded as refreshes, and Power BI and BO retention windows too short to see annual cycles. Every gap is recorded in USAGE_SUMMARY per platform with the observed window, so a rule that cites usage also cites what the usage could not have seen.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-23', 'Pitfalls that recur in BI migrations', 'method', $$REF-23 — Pitfalls that recur in BI migrations: these are the failures the workbench is designed to prevent, listed so that an engineer recognizes them when a shortcut is proposed. Migrating dead reports: converting the whole estate because the assessment was skipped or not trusted, which doubles the cost and moves the clutter. The rule engine exists so that every asset has a disposition before anyone opens a conversion ticket. Trusting titles: treating "Executive Dashboard" as important and "test_copy_v3" as junk, when readership and lineage say the opposite; titles are inputs to INVESTIGATE, never to a disposition. Converting parameters literally: turning a Tableau parameter that drove a calculation into a Sigma control of the same name without checking whether the calculation still needs it, when a grouping level or a column in the certified dataset has made it unnecessary. Ignoring subscriptions: retiring a report with zero views that three directors receive in their inbox every Monday, then discovering it on a Monday. Not freezing the source during reconciliation: comparing a Sigma number against a Tableau extract that refreshed overnight, chasing a variance that is a data timing difference, and then "fixing" the conversion to match a stale number. Fixing a conversion to match a wrong source number at all (REF-16). Letting the model invent a function: accepting an AI-produced formula that references a Sigma function that does not exist or a column that was not in the dataset, because it looked plausible; the function list and column list checks exist for this. Certifying by consultant: recording a metric definition as certified because the engineer chose the most common variant, when certification is a named business owner's act. Skipping wave 0: starting subject-area waves before conformed dimensions are certified, and rebuilding the patient dimension three times. Closing a wave with open acceptances because the date arrived. Each of these has a table, a status, or a gate in the workbench that makes the shortcut visible; the pitfall is taking the shortcut anyway.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-24', 'Owner notification and the silence window', 'rationalization', $$REF-24 — Owner notification and the silence window: a RETIRE or CONSOLIDATE recommendation does not advance to a reviewer until the asset's owner has been notified and a fixed silence window, 30 days by default in RULE_CONFIG, has elapsed without an objection. The owner is resolved from the platform (Tableau workbook owner, Power BI report owner or workspace admin, BO document owner), and when the owner has left the organization, which is common for exactly the assets that are candidates for retirement, the notification goes to the owner's manager from the HR feed and to the department's analytics lead, and the asset is flagged ORPHANED in the evidence JSON. The notification is generated from a template that the copilot can draft and a human sends: it names the asset, states the evidence in plain terms (no interactive readers since a date, no subscriptions found, not on the obligation register), says what will happen and when, and gives one action to stop it, which is to reply with who reads it and why. An objection converts the recommendation to INVESTIGATE with the objection text attached, and the reviewer decides; an objection is not automatically a reprieve, because "I might need it someday" is the reason estates grow, but it is always a conversation. Silence is treated as consent only because the window is long, the notification is recorded with a timestamp in NOTIFICATIONS, and the retirement is reversible during the read-only period that follows (REF-18). The window is not a formality to compress when the schedule slips. The cost of a 30-day wait on an asset nobody reads is nothing; the cost of retiring a report that a payer contract manager reads quarterly is a missed reconciliation. When a wave's schedule needs assets to be decided faster, the correct lever is to start notifications earlier, in the assessment phase, not to shorten the window.$$);
INSERT INTO AI.MIGRATION_REFERENCE VALUES ('REF-25', 'Migrating off a hosted analytics platform (MedeAnalytics and similar)', 'conversion', $$REF-25 — Migrating off a hosted analytics platform (MedeAnalytics and similar): a hosted healthcare analytics platform such as MedeAnalytics is not a BI tool over your warehouse; it is a warehouse and a BI tool together, run by the vendor. That changes three things about the migration. First, inventory: there is no public metadata or usage API. The report catalog, report definitions, user activity and scheduled deliveries come from the vendor's admin console exports and from the vendor's services team, and their exact columns must be confirmed per customer; the INVENTORY shapes for MEDE_* tables are illustrative until they are. Second, lineage: a report reads vendor data domains (an accounts domain, a denials domain, a members domain) that the vendor built from your 837/835, ADT and eligibility feeds. Lineage stops at the domain column; it does not reach your source system. The source catalog therefore maps each domain to a conformed entity at domain grain, and the target model's source for those entities is your own system of record, not the vendor domain — which means the first wave for these assets is a data-engineering wave. Third, and most important, metrics: most standard measures are VENDOR-DEFINED. Denial rate, clean claim rate, expected reimbursement, risk score and benchmark percentile are computed by the vendor's logic, sometimes against vendor benchmark sets or proprietary models, and the definition is not in the report. The translator classifies these VENDOR_DEFINED, scores them as complexity, and routes the asset to REBUILD with a reason that says so. The right sequence is: obtain the measure dictionary entry from the vendor; decide with the business whether to reproduce the vendor's definition or adopt your certified one (they will differ, and the reconciliation harness will show DEFINITION_VARIANCE when they do); certify the definition on the target model; then build the Sigma workbook against it. Custom report-builder fields, by contrast, use a simple SUM / COUNT / IF / DAYS syntax and convert mechanically. Proprietary models (risk scores, benchmarks) may have no equivalent at all: the honest disposition is to decide whether the organization still needs them, and if so, to source them from a replacement (a risk adjustment vendor, a benchmark subscription) rather than pretend the workbook can compute them. Decommissioning a hosted platform also ends the vendor's data feeds, so the cutover plan must confirm that every domain the surviving reports depend on has a live system-of-record mapping before the contract lapses.$$);
